{"records":[{"id":"mmz2gznth5","title":"The phosphodiesterase 2A controls lymphatic junctional maturation via cGMP-mediated control of Notch signaling. Carlantoni et al. ","description":"The molecular mechanisms by which lymphatic vessels induce cell contact inhibition are not understood. Here, we identify the cGMP-dependent phosphodiesterase 2A (PDE2A) as a selective regulator of lymphatic, but not blood endothelial contact inhibition. Conditional deletion of Pde2a in mouse embryos reveals severe lymphatic dysplasia, while blood vessel architecture remains unaltered. In the absence of PDE2A, human lymphatic endothelial cells fail to induce mature junctions and cell cycle arrest, while cGMP levels, but not cAMP levels, are increased. Loss of PDE2A-mediated cGMP hydrolysis leads to activation of p38 signaling and downregulation of NOTCH signaling. Vice versa, DLL4-induced NOTCH activation restores junctional maturation and contact inhibition in PDE2A-deficient lymphatic endothelial cells. In postnatal mesenteries, PDE2A is specifically enriched in collecting lymphatic valves and loss of Pde2a results in formation of abnormal valves. Our data demonstrate that PDE2A selectively finetunes a crosstalk of cGMP, p38 and NOTCH signaling during lymphatic vessel maturation.\n\nSource data\nOriginal western blot images of the main figures ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:45:40.116Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmz2gznth5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmz2gznth5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maike","last_name":"Frye","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Endothelial Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2wkphfrn84","title":"SPNS2 enables T cell egress from lymph nodes during an immune response","description":"T cell expression of sphingosine 1-phosphate receptor 1 (S1PR1) enables T cell exit from lymph nodes (LN) into lymph, while endothelial S1PR1 expression regulates vascular permeability. Drugs targeting S1PR1 treat autoimmune disease by trapping pathogenic T cells within LN, but have adverse cardiovascular side-effects. In homeostasis, the transporter SPNS2 supplies lymph S1P and enables T cell exit, while the transporter MFSD2B supplies most blood S1P and supports vascular function. It is unknown whether SPNS2 remains necessary to supply lymph S1P during an immune response, or whether in inflammation other compensatory transporters are up- regulated. Here, using a model of dermal inflammation, we demonstrate that SPNS2 supplies the S1P that guides T cells out of LN with an ongoing immune response. Furthermore, deletion of Spns2 is protective in a mouse model of multiple sclerosis.These results support the therapeutic potential of SPNS2 inhibitors to achieve spatially specific modulation of S1P signaling.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-17T13:55:25.131Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2wkphfrn84","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2wkphfrn84"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Martyna","last_name":"Okuniewska","orcid":"0000-0003-0429-4854"},{"first_name":"Victoria","last_name":"Fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Audrey","last_name":"Baeyens","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Varsha","last_name":"Raghavan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"June-Yong","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dan","last_name":"Littman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Susan R.","last_name":"Schwab","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Autoimmune Disorder","Cell Migration","T Cell","Mouse Model","Experimental Autoimmune Encephalomyelitis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mwtgvwwjh5","title":"Tumor-Derived EVs Promote Lymph Node Metastasis in ESCC  by Targeting SUMO1","description":"Protein profiles of metastatic lymph nodes and negative lymph nodes in esophageal cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-05T12:55:44.861Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mwtgvwwjh5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mwtgvwwjh5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yanli","last_name":"cai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tumor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2zgppyr2rr","title":"Dynamic CD8+ T cell responses to cancer immunotherapy in human regional lymph nodes are disrupted in metastatic lymph nodes","description":"Data related to the paper: \"Dynamic CD8+ T cell responses to cancer immunotherapy in human regional lymph nodes are disrupted in metastatic lymph nodes,\" Cell (2023)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-06T16:43:51.869Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2zgppyr2rr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2zgppyr2rr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maha","last_name":"Rahim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Trine Line","last_name":"Okholm","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kyle","last_name":"Jones","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"McCarthy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Candace","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jacqueline","last_name":"Yee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stanley","last_name":"Tamaki","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Diana","last_name":"Marquez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Iliana","last_name":"Tenvooren","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katherine","last_name":"Wai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Cheung","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brittany","last_name":"Davidson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vrinda","last_name":"Johri","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bushra","last_name":"Samad","orcid":null},{"first_name":"William","last_name":"O'Gorman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Krummel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Annemieke","last_name":"van Zante","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Combes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Angelo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lawrence","last_name":"Fong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alain","last_name":"Algazi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patrick","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Spitzer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Cancer Immunology","Cancer Immunotherapy","Lymph Node","Cytotoxic T-Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vrpgfc99w6","title":"Combined features","description":"This study utilized data on the status of station 4  lymph nodes in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) spanning January 2017 to January 2024. Radiomics features were extracted from regions of interest (ROIs) in station 4 lymph nodes across pN0-pN2 stages, derived from venous-phase CT imaging, and integrated with clinically relevant features collected from hospital records to develop a multimodal predictive model. The combined model aims to improve the accuracy of predicting metastasis in NSCLC station 4 lymph nodes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-10T22:01:44.463Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vrpgfc99w6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vrpgfc99w6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yanru","last_name":"Kang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Radiomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wggskc33f3","title":"2023 BMEL LEC Bioreactor Data","description":"Source data used in the paper \"A Bioreactor for In Vitro Studies of Lymphatic Endothelial Cells with Simultaneous Fluid Shear Stress and Membrane Strain\". See file \"001 - Data Map.pdf\" for more info on datasets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-18T16:34:18.855Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wggskc33f3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wggskc33f3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Jastram","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caleb","last_name":"Davis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Shape Analysis","Fluorescence Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ww4nnpm4kj","title":"Imaging Superficial Cervical Lymph Nodes in the EAE MS Model using CEST MRI, MALDI","description":"CEST MRI, flow cytometry, MALDI data of superficial cervical lymph nodes in EAE mice over the course of the model","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-13T14:15:55.639Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ww4nnpm4kj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ww4nnpm4kj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aline","last_name":"Thomas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Imaging","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Multiple Sclerosis","Matrix-Assisted Laser Desorption-Ionization"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dbszz6wbdy","title":"supplement files for ‘Dysfunction and Pathological Origins of Lymphatic Endothelial Cells in  Atherosclerosis Revealed by Single-Cell Transcriptomics’","description":"Our study leverages single-cell RNA sequencing to deconstruct the heterogeneity and dynamic functional changes of lymphatic endothelial cells (LECs) during the progression of atherosclerosis. A key mechanistic insight is the discovery of a disease-specific transdifferentiation path from fibroblasts to LECs, a finding that reveals a previously unknown source of lymphatic endothelium in the pathological context. Furthermore, we detail how LECs undergo functional reprogramming, shifting from immune tolerance to pro-inflammatory activation and concurrently losing their lipid-clearing capacity, thereby contributing to disease pathogenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-11T21:56:06.072Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dbszz6wbdy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dbszz6wbdy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"qinhang","last_name":"shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Atherosclerosis","Lymphatic Vessel"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3ntbp3zdzh","title":"The normal Lymph Node. Supplemental Data","description":"Normal human lymph nodes and tonsils, multiplexed with the MILAN technique, analyzed with BRAQUE.","source":{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-12T12:26:44.144Z","url":"https://board.unimib.it/datasets/3ntbp3zdzh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3ntbp3zdzh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maddalena","last_name":"Bolognesi","orcid":"0000-0002-8238-2369"},{"first_name":"Lorenzo","last_name":"Dall'Olio","orcid":"0000-0002-8907-0034"},{"first_name":"Simone","last_name":"Borghesi","orcid":"0000-0001-8145-2364"},{"first_name":"gastone","last_name":"castellani","orcid":"0000-0003-4892-925X"},{"first_name":"Giorgio","last_name":"Cattoretti","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/020dw9k11","name":"Regione Lombardia","grant_id":"POR FESR 2014–2020"}],"categories":["Image Classification Techniques","Tissue Cell","Image Classification","Lymphoid Tissue","Image Analysis","Bioinformatics Software","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9m25b33zyk","title":"The impact of coexisting Hashimoto's thyroiditis on the feasibility of endoscopic thyroidectomy in papillary thyroid carcinoma.","description":"These files demonstrate the number of dissected lymph nodes, mean operation time, thyroid volume, blood loss, TSH level, tumor size in 110 patients with PTC .","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-23T08:01:22.502Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9m25b33zyk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9m25b33zyk"],"contributors":[{"f